WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.115 --> 00:00:06.535
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.535 --> 00:00:10.867
We are now ready to learn how biologists
compare sequences.

00:00:10.867 --> 00:00:17.750
And we'll start from something that, again,
seemingly has nothing to do with biology.

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:20.020
It's called the alignment game.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:24.860
We are given two sequences, and we can do
the following things.

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:29.040
We can either remove the first symbols
from both sequences.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.770
And in this case, if these two symbols

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:37.124
are the same, we get a score of one.
If they are different, we get nothing.

00:00:37.124 --> 00:00:43.113
Alternatively, we can remove a single
symbol from one of the sequences.

00:00:43.113 --> 00:00:44.522
Let's start.

00:00:44.522 --> 00:00:47.810
So, this is how a sequence is and we got
lucky.

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:50.140
They both start from A.

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:54.180
We remove A from both sequences, and we
get one point.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:57.040
Now, the sequences start from T,

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:00.820
once again, we remove both T, and we get
another point.

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:05.430
Next, the sequences start from G and C, so
we can either remove

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:08.760
both, but we'll get no point, or we can
remove one of them.

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:10.240
That's what we do.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:13.360
And after we removed C from the second
sequence, 

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:17.740
we actually have two sequences
starting from G.

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:21.350
That's good.
We can remove both and get one more point.

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:21.930
We continue,

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:26.370
one more point, continue, continue,
continue, and finally,

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:29.780
we finish doing the game, and got 4
points.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:35.180
But maybe, if we played a different
strategy, we could get 5 points.

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:42.260
But before we decide how to play
this game.

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:44.600
Let's define the fundamental

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:47.560
bioinformatics notion of sequence
alignment.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:51.210
We didn't notice it, but while we were
playing

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:54.770
the game, we actually constructed, the
alignment of two sequences.

00:01:55.880 --> 00:02:00.130
Alignment of two sequences is a two-row
matrix.

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:03.840
In the first row, we have symbols from the
first

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:08.980
sequence in order.
Possibly interspaced by the space symbol.

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:13.920
In the second row, we have symbols from
the second sequence.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:18.230
Once again in order, possibly interspaced
by the space symbol.

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:24.070
And that's what's shown on this slide.
They further classify different columns

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:28.890
of the alignment matrix as matches.
These are the columns for which we

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:32.190
get score one with identical letters.

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:37.130
Or mismatches, this is a column where we
have two letters but they are different.

00:02:37.130 --> 00:02:42.335
And we also have insertions and deletions,
sometimes collectively called indels.

00:02:42.335 --> 00:02:47.880
So, what we known is that the maximum

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:53.800
score in the alignment game is actually
the maximum number of matches,

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:55.730
is alignment matrix.

00:02:55.730 --> 00:02:58.330
It doesn't help us, to solve either the

00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:01.990
alignment problem or to play optimally the
alignment game.

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:05.450
But we will see later how to do this.

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:09.530
We will also define the notion of common
subsequence.

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:13.970
Common subsequence between two sequences
is simply the matches

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:16.400
in the alignment of these two sequences.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:19.090
And a classical problem in Computer
Science

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:23.330
is called the longest common subsequence
problem.

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:29.630
Given two strings, we want to find a
longest common subsequence of two strings.

00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:32.250
This problem will be the key for

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:36.220
our understanding of how biologists
compare by sequences.

